# Copy to .env (gitignored, never committed) and fill in a real value to
# enable server/index.mjs's server-side hermes-gateway proxy (GET
# /gateway-status, POST /diagnose) — see README.md's "Human-in-the-loop
# review + self-improvement loop" / dashboard section, and
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-21-autonomous-layout-agent-design.md.
#
# Pattern: same as ~/gitspace/mi-report's profile .env (MI_LLM_API_KEY) —
# the server loads this automatically on startup (process.loadEnvFile()),
# so the browser never has to handle the key at all. Without it, the
# dashboard's Diagnosis page falls back to asking for a pasted key
# instead (still works, just less convenient).
#
# One of the values from GATEWAY_CLIENT_KEYS in the hermes-gateway
# instance's own .env (e.g. ~/gitspace/AIFde/.env), NOT a value to
# invent — this project doesn't run its own gateway.
PPA_EDA_GATEWAY_KEY=

# --- optional: a plain-model route for work that isn't EDA analysis ---
#
# Every hermes-gateway model is somebody's persona. ppa-eda-analyst
# spends ~19,100 prompt tokens before it reads any input (measured: a
# two-letter prompt still bills 19,129, and a 17-character translation
# still took 9.2s), and it reasons rather than just answering —
# translating the real 8,744-char sram_wrapper diagnosis produced 9,843
# completion tokens for roughly 3,500 tokens of actual Korean, so about
# two thirds of a ~200s wait was the analyst thinking about chip design.
#
# Translation needs a language model, not a PPA analyst. Set these and
# POST /translate uses them instead; leave them empty and everything
# behaves exactly as before, through the gateway.
#
# Same idea as ~/gitspace/lsi_error_analyzer, which drives OpenRouter
# directly rather than through a persona gateway. Any OpenAI-compatible
# endpoint works — this server already speaks that wire format, so no
# new dependency is involved.
PPA_EDA_DIRECT_LLM_KEY=
PPA_EDA_DIRECT_LLM_MODEL=
# Defaults to https://openrouter.ai/api/v1 when unset.
PPA_EDA_DIRECT_LLM_BASE_URL=
